{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1780015008,"nonce":"xvO7CnbxrEKbSCj73fTaC5wSncs=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABEFwpYg","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/183#issuecomment-4569442658"},"message":"Fresh on-water motivation from deployment #186 (2026-05-28) — this moved from \"CA is invisible\" to a concrete operability problem.\n\nIn a dense-buoy / dark-sky region the reflex collision avoidance (pointcloud-fed) fired repeatedly — **331 `collision_monitor` state transitions**, stop/slowdown alternating every 0.2–1.5 s — chopping `cmd_vel`. The boat **orbited a buoy** (planner produced detours, CA cancelled forward progress before completion → crab-orbit) and was at one point **\"mostly paralyzed by a close target,\"** cleared only by a manual USB-controller nudge.\n\nCrucially, the operator had **no in-CAMP indication** of *what* CA was reacting to or *when* it was in SLOWDOWN vs STOP. Root-causing it required reading `~/.ros/log/collision_monitor*.log` on the boat-side host after the fact. This is exactly the gap this issue addresses: the pointcloud + polygons + `collision_monitor_state` overlay would have shown the trigger storm live, and the annunciator row would make \"the boat stopped *because of CA*\" obvious in the moment.\n\nWithout it the operator is blind to the CA trigger storm in exactly the dense-obstacle conditions where it matters most — operability-blocking for real survey work in cluttered areas.\n\n(Detail in the #186 gabby per-host log §17 / §22 / §23.)\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.8`","files":null}]}